+# The October 15, 2003 cooperation directive and the FAA production review
+
+November 9, 2026 · B129 / PD04 / PD07. Four archived copies preserve the White House counsel’s October 15 memorandum on cooperation with the Commission. It asks agencies to review their searches, names the executive-branch liaison and describes the production issues at the agency subject to a subpoena vote as **“now largely resolved.”** The Commission’s same-day statement says its staff had yet to finish reviewing the FAA material. An October 14 draft minutes packet also records a planned two-week compliance check. The actual review reports and recipient-side records remain open. [Commission statement](https://govinfo.library.unt.edu/911/press/pr_2003-10-15.pdf) · [Exact archive sources and locators](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## Instructions and competing accounts of production
+
+| Record | What the selected pages show |
+| --- | --- |
+| **March 19, 2003 Card memorandum**, Marcus item 224 p. 5 | Names the **Secretary of Transportation** among the addressees and **Adam Ciongoli** as the liaison. It asks each recipient to designate a senior point of contact and notify the Attorney General in writing by **March 21, 2003**. A signed-page reproduction follows the October correspondence in the archive packet. The October message lists a Card PDF. The actual named attachment and each agency’s written designation remain to be identified. |
+| **October 14, 2003 draft minutes**, Kojm Box 2, pp. 1–6 | Two versions report that FAA had produced **approximately 20 boxes late on October 10**. They attribute to Team 8’s leader uncertainty about whether the production fulfilled the request. The decisions paragraph on pp. 3 and 6 records an FAA subpoena without dissent and calls for teams to test agencies’ compliance over the **next two weeks** and report to the General Counsel. Both record a discussion about informing Gonzales the next morning before a public statement. The copy on p. 6 has added handwriting below the printed decision. The annotation’s authorship, timing and status are unresolved. |
+| **October 15, 2003 Gonzales memorandum**, Marcus item 224 pp. 3–4 and three other copies | Names the **Secretary of Transportation** among the addressees, copies heads of executive agencies, and asks agencies to review their previous responses, conduct complete searches and work with **Daniel Levin**. It describes a vote to subpoena one agency and gives the **“now largely resolved”** assessment without naming that agency in the passage. The Commission’s same-day statement and the signed subpoena identify FAA. |
+| **October 15 Commission statement and October 16 signed subpoena** | The statement describes receipt of **dozens of boxes and materials** and says staff had yet to review them fully. It says the Commission voted to issue an FAA subpoena. The signed instrument bears the separate **October 16** date. These are distinct accounts and event records. The approximately 20 boxes, dozens of boxes and memorandum’s executive-wide **more than 2,100,000 pages** are different reported quantities and populations. No item-level reconciliation was performed. [Statement, p. 1](https://govinfo.library.unt.edu/911/press/pr_2003-10-15.pdf#page=1) · [Signed instrument, p. 1](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-AWA-810-Natl-Commission-Subpoena-to-the-FAA.pdf#page=1) |
+
+The draft minutes’ account of a pending review, the counsel memorandum’s assessment, and the Commission’s public description remain side by side. An agency’s submission could have answered some outstanding concerns while staff still had review work to do. That possibility is untested. The dated boxes and itemized inventories, actual receipt and staff review reports would distinguish it from other explanations.
+
+## Distribution fields as preserved
+
+- **Marcus item 224 p. 3** displays an October 15 message at **14:51:57 −0400**. It lists `Memo from Card re Commission.pdf` and places the Gonzales text below the message. The March 19 Card reproduction is on p. 5. The first two pages reproduce a separate **February 13, 2004** Card memorandum on pre-publication review. Keep the container order, dates and subjects distinct. No native attachment is embedded in the tested PDF. [Item 224 locators and hash](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+- **Hamilton 02310** displays **5:22 PM**. Marcus says the text circulated within the executive branch, had yet to be made public and that he planned to send it to commissioners. **Hamilton 02313** displays **7:53 PM** and describes a White House plan to make the memo public. Its second canvas has markings around the search and status language. Both scans preserve statements about dissemination, with no confirmed agency receipt or public release date. [02310 manifest](https://digitalcollections.iu.edu/concern/archival_materials/wp989854t/manifest) · [02313 manifest](https://digitalcollections.iu.edu/concern/archival_materials/nk3233864/manifest)
+- **Team 3 Box 5** preserves a forward with **20:01:58 −0400** and a nested message with **19:53:39 −0400**. The nested wording about intended publicity corresponds to the selected Hamilton passage. Preserve the timestamps as printed and treat the memo in these contexts as one underlying document. A forwarded scan leaves agency-by-agency dispatch, acknowledgment and internal tasking open. [Team 3 member and scope](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
